  "analysis": "The patient exhibits symptoms consistent with Frey's syndrome, which is a common complication following parotid gland surgery, particularly after procedures like extrafacial parotidectomy. The symptoms of pain during mastication, sweating, and reddening of the skin in the preauricular area align with this diagnosis. The standard treatment for Frey's syndrome is the injection of botulinum toxin, which has been shown to effectively reduce sweating and alleviate discomfort in the affected area. Total parotidectomy is not indicated unless there is a suspicion of tumor recurrence, which is not suggested in this case. Pregabalin is primarily used for neuropathic pain and would not directly address the symptoms of Frey's syndrome. Broad-spectrum antibiotics are not appropriate as there is no indication of infection. Therefore, the most appropriate and effective treatment option is the injection of intradermal botulinum toxin, as it directly targets the symptoms and is less invasive than surgical options.",
